# ADR-006: Asynchronous Host Execution

## Status

Proposed

---

## Context

The first production Host (GitHub Runtime Host) executes the complete Engineering Workflow synchronously as part of webhook processing.

Current execution flow:

GitHub Pull Request

↓

Webhook Verification

↓

Workspace Preparation

↓

RuntimeHost.execute()

↓

Engineering Workflow

↓

GitHub Comment

↓

HTTP Response

This approach is appropriate for initial validation because it:

- minimizes operational complexity;
- validates the Runtime architecture end-to-end;
- simplifies debugging;
- avoids introducing infrastructure before it is required.

However, production Engineering Workflows may perform:

- repository preparation;
- static analysis;
- multiple LLM invocations;
- repository-wide analysis;
- future autonomous Engineering tasks.

Execution time therefore becomes unpredictable.

GitHub recommends webhook handlers acknowledge delivery quickly. Long-running executions increase the likelihood of request timeouts, retries, duplicate deliveries, and reduced system resilience.

The Runtime Execution Core itself is already independent of transport and request lifecycles.

Only the Host execution model is affected.

---

## Decision

Production Hosts should support asynchronous execution.

The Runtime Execution Core will remain completely synchronous.

Hosts become responsible for scheduling Runtime executions rather than executing them directly inside inbound request handlers.

The Runtime continues exposing exactly one execution entry point:

RuntimeHost.execute()

No queueing, retries, scheduling, or worker concepts will be introduced into the Runtime.

---

## Synchronous Execution

Suitable for:

- local development;
- testing;
- CLI execution;
- demonstrations;
- debugging.

Execution:

Webhook

↓

RuntimeHost.execute()

↓

HTTP Response

---

## Asynchronous Execution

Production execution should follow:

Webhook

↓

Verify Event

↓

Persist Execution Request

↓

Return HTTP 202 Accepted

↓

Queue

↓

Worker

↓

Workspace Preparation

↓

CapabilityComposition

↓

RuntimeHost.execute()

↓

Publish Results

The queue implementation is intentionally outside the Runtime architecture.

Hosts may use:

- in-memory queues;
- cloud messaging systems;
- task schedulers;
- background workers;
- event buses;

without affecting Runtime behavior.

---

## Responsibilities

### Runtime

Responsible only for:

- planning;
- execution;
- artifact routing;
- capability resolution.

The Runtime remains unaware of:

- queues;
- retries;
- workers;
- scheduling;
- delivery acknowledgements.

---

### Host

Responsible for:

- receiving provider events;
- acknowledging requests;
- deduplicating deliveries if necessary;
- scheduling execution;
- invoking RuntimeHost.execute();
- publishing workflow results.

---

### Worker

Responsible for:

- obtaining scheduled executions;
- preparing infrastructure;
- creating CapabilityComposition;
- executing RuntimeHost;
- reporting failures.

Workers remain Host infrastructure.

---

## Consequences

### Advantages

- Fast webhook acknowledgement.
- Better production reliability.
- Improved scalability.
- Horizontal execution.
- Retry support.
- Independent execution lifetime.
- Multiple concurrent Engineering Workflows.

### Trade-offs

- Increased operational complexity.
- Queue infrastructure required.
- Worker deployment required.
- Eventual consistency between webhook reception and published results.
- Execution monitoring becomes necessary.

---

## Validation

The synchronous GitHub Runtime Host implemented in Epic 11 validates the Runtime architecture and serves as the reference implementation.

Future production deployments may introduce asynchronous execution without modifying:

- Runtime Execution Core;
- Planner;
- Execution Engine;
- Workflows;
- Artifacts;
- Capabilities;
- Runtime Host SPI.

Only Host infrastructure changes.

This confirms that execution scheduling is an operational concern rather than a Runtime concern.